<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tzitzak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 750</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Irene of Khazaria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tzitzak was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> princess, a daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, who in 732 married the future Byzantine emperor Constantine V and was baptised as Irene. The marriage sealed an alliance between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and the Khazars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>. Their son reigned as Leo IV from 775 to 780 and was known as &quot;the Khazar&quot;, and her wedding dress gave its name to a Byzantine court garment, the tzitzakion.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Tzitzak, in Greek Τζιτζάκ, was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>. Her name is most widely thought to be a Hellenized form of a Turkic word derived from Proto-Turkic čeček, related to Chuvash and Turkish words meaning flower. Marcel Erdal, however, notes that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> used tzitzak for the empress's garment and finds the idea that it was her personal name far-fetched. He thinks the word more likely described the colourfulness of the garment, and he compares the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> ṣiṣiṯ, the fringed Jewish ceremonial shawl.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 732 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> was threatened with invasion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>. Seeking allies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-iii-the-isaurian">Leo III the Isaurian</a> sent an embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, and the alliance was sealed by the marriage of Tzitzak to Constantine V, Leo's son and junior co-ruler. She was escorted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Constantine was about fourteen, and Tzitzak may have been even younger, since she did not give birth for eighteen years. She became a Christian under the baptismal name Irene. Her wedding gown started a fashion in Constantinople for male robes called tzitzakia.</p><p>Leo III died on 18 June 741 and Constantine V succeeded him, with Irene as empress. Civil war broke out almost at once, when Artabasdos, Constantine's brother-in-law, claimed the throne. It lasted until 2 November 743, and the chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophanes-the-confessor">Theophanes the Confessor</a> does not describe any part Irene played in it. Theophanes records that she learned to read religious texts, and that she lived piously, which he contrasts with the impiety of her father-in-law and husband. Both emperors were iconoclasts, while Theophanes was an iconodule monk, and his praise probably reflects that Irene shared his views.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 25 January 750 Constantine and Tzitzak had a son, Leo, who succeeded his father as Emperor Leo IV and is better known as Leo the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a>. His birth is the last mention of Irene in the historical record. By the following year Constantine was already married to his second wife, Maria. Lynda Garland has suggested that Tzitzak died in childbirth, which would fit the death c. 750 given for her.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tzitzak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Byzantine emperor Constantine V married the Khazar princess <b>Tzitzak</b>, and their son reigned as Leo IV, known as 'the Khazar'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Leo, whose byname came from his Khazar mother <b>Tzitzak</b>, reigned until his death in 780.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-14">14 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
